```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
};

export function Card({ children, onActivate }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="@container w-full max-w-screen-lg border border-brand bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-sm">
      <div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 @lg:grid-cols-3">
        <div className="min-w-0 text-lg @lg:col-span-2">
          {children}
        </div>

        <aside className="text-sm text-ink/70">
          sidebar
        </aside>
      </div>

      <button
        type="button"
        className="mt-6 text-brand focus-visible:outline-brand"
        onClick={onActivate}
      >
        Open card
      </button>
    </article>
  );
}
```

How each finding is addressed:

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: remove `onClick` from the non-semantic `<div>` and expose the action through a real `<button>`. It receives keyboard activation and screen-reader semantics without manually recreating them with `role`, `tabIndex`, and key handlers. If activation navigates somewhere, use `<a href>` instead.
- `design_token_escape`: replace inline hex, RGB, shadow, spacing, and outline literals with theme-backed utilities such as `bg-surface`, `text-ink`, `border-brand`, `p-6`, and `shadow-sm`. These build on the tokens already declared in `tailwind.config.ts`.
- `responsive_fragility`: remove the fixed `800px`, `720px`, `600px`, and `240px` dimensions. The card becomes `w-full` with a tokenized maximum width; content uses a fluid grid that stacks by default and expands through a container-query variant. Typography uses the configured scale rather than raw pixel sizes.

Keeping the interactive control separate also avoids placing arbitrary `children`—which might themselves contain links or buttons—inside a clickable card. Since `crimes context` found no likely tests, I’d add coverage for keyboard activation, accessible role/name, and narrow-container layout before implementing this refactor.
